{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central claim, that a single large service 'centralizes the control plane, meaning a single coordinated decision (e.g., a bad config push) can defeat all N redundant components,' assumes the monolith is naïvely architected. The problem explicitly posits a monolith with 'strictly more total internal redundancy.' If that redundancy includes functional separation of control planes (e.g., redundant deployment regions with independent configuration controllers and software stacks, a standard feature in highly available monoliths), the 'control-plane coordination' trap the target builds its argument on vanishes: a bad config push cannot propagate to all redundant components. The target even acknowledges this as a countercondition ('strictly isolated, independently verifiable control planes'), but its main mechanism/claim still treats centralization of control as an inherent property of the monolith. This makes the target's conditional defense of decentralization non-general: it only holds for a specific class of monolith that lacks control-plane isolation, not for the maximally redundant monolith described by the problem, so the argument does not actually establish that a decentralized network is more resilient under the stated comparison.","target":"e1011551a55de9d532c6ac7d72f22e86b94934a59bf48296ce5bb31b986de49d"}]}